From 2ade4c228e495b7e7dd51b1fbac5a29c69f06419 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:34:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(interaction): retain native held block-use sessions

Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5 retains StartItemUseOn through physical use holds.
Transport that lifetime from aware Java clients, distinguish first input
from simulation repeats, and stop on release or capability withdrawal.
Ordinary clients keep independent uses.

Prediction format 2 matches placement, interaction, failure and a native
cursor to the concluding Java sequence. Held trapdoors retain their first
world hit even outside later target cubes. Reject non-finite coordinates
and stale sequence ownership without changing the Java raycast packet.

Start the session only after predicted success. Native Survival predicts
one consumed block. Send its remaining stack in the first use-in-air
phase only when the client admits that phase. Use a legacy request while the predicted stack remains nonempty;
consuming the last block uses ID zero and skips the empty use-in-air phase.
Creative placements, failed attempts and ordinary interactions carry no
placement delta. Retain predicted counts until authoritative correction,
and do not echo unchanged equipment after confirmation.

Use one inventory-owned even legacy ID sequence for block placement and
other legacy transactions. Keep odd stack requests independent. Emit
native build/interact swings and preserve subsequent Java attack packets:
Java 26.3 right-click swings stay local and cannot reserve the next PUNCH.

Fresh native fixtures force the client into Survival and verify its item
predictions. Earlier captures combined a Creative native default with
server Survival consumption and did not establish Survival inventory
format. Native camera/use ordering also affects authoritative facing;
retain server correction instead of forcing the predicted orientation.

Focused tests cover lifetimes, result/cursor ownership, clock-independent
sequence handling, failed attempts, native use phases and inventory
prediction boundaries. Captures and proprietary resources stay private.

The extra first use-in-air phase follows placement, not block activation.
A fresh trapdoor activation consumes the interaction without calling the
block item's air use. Failed use-on attempts retain Java's ordinary air
fallback rather than synthesizing another phase.

Fresh native block dispatch is independent of the strict 200 ms air
clock. A verified current-build fresh callback dispatches rejected block
use 27 ms before that clock expires, without another air transaction.
The same source gates the air phase after successful placement. Do not
synthesize an air packet solely because a fresh placement succeeded.

Negotiate prediction format 3 on a distinct optional channel. Carry air
admission with the sequence-owned result and cursor. A matched false
admission suppresses only the extra air transaction, preserving build
prediction, inventory consumption and session start. Keep format 2 on
its existing channel for older clients, with its prior air default.
Withdraw either prediction capability through the existing held owner.
Tests cover the legacy wire fixture, the suppressed-air codec, sequence
ownership and build success independently from the air decision.

Pair ordinary item-use consumption prediction with its concluding Java
sequence. Keep one optional report and retire it after that packet or
capability withdrawal. A skipped sequence must not leak its prediction
into a later use. Sequence numbering can restart with a new client level.

Native Bedrock1.26.60.5 records an inventory action only when local use
changes the stack. Cooldown-refused pearl callbacks dispatch without
a debit or legacy slot declaration. Publish that distinction before the
ordinary Java packet so inventory translation can retain native behavior
without using Java carrier classes or changing air-route ownership.
